lessons.explanation:
## Describing object shapes

Most bugs in CRUD apps are shape bugs: a function expects `{ id, title }` and receives `{ id, name }`. An **interface** names a shape once, and every use is checked:

```ts
interface User {
  id: number;
  name: string;
  email?: string;          // ? = optional: string | undefined
  readonly createdAt: string;  // cannot be reassigned after creation
}

function displayName(user: User): string {
  return user.name;            // ✓ checked property access
  // return user.nmae;         // ✗ compile error: typo caught!
}

const u: User = { id: 1, name: 'Ada', createdAt: '2024-01-01' };
// email omitted — fine, it's optional
```

Line by line: each property gets a name and type. The `?` on `email` makes it optional — omitting it is legal, and reading it gives `string | undefined`, forcing you to handle the missing case. `readonly` forbids assignment after creation (`u.createdAt = '...'` is a compile error) — note this is **compile-time only**; the emitted JavaScript object is still mutable.

TypeScript is **structural**: any object with the right properties satisfies the interface, no `implements` declaration needed. Functions taking typed objects are where interfaces shine — the parameter annotation documents and enforces the whole contract at once.

### Common mistakes

- Using an optional property without handling `undefined` — that's the bug `?` exists to surface.
- Expecting `readonly` to protect objects at runtime. It doesn't; it's a compiler rule.
- Writing `user: object` — that says "some object", checking nothing useful. Name the shape.

## Functions describing functions

Callbacks are values, so they have types too. A **function type** spells out parameters and return: `(n: number) => boolean` is "takes a number, gives a boolean":

```ts
function countMatching(nums: number[], predicate: (n: number) => boolean): number {
  let count = 0;
  for (const n of nums) {
    if (predicate(n)) count++;
  }
  return count;
}
countMatching([1, 2, 3, 4], (n) => n % 2 === 0);   // 2 — n is inferred as number!
```

Line by line: the second parameter's type is itself a signature. At the call site, the arrow function's `n` needs no annotation — TypeScript infers it from the expected callback type (*contextual typing*). Pass `(s) => s.toUpperCase()` instead and the compiler objects: strings don't come out of a `number[]`.

Parameters can be **optional** (`port?: number` — becomes `number | undefined`) or **defaulted** (`greeting = 'Hello'` — type inferred, caller may omit). Optional/defaulted parameters must come after required ones.

Two special return types: `void` means "returns nothing useful — don't use the result"; `never` means "never returns at all" (always throws, or loops forever).

### Common mistakes

- Writing `callback: Function` — that's the `any` of function types; spell out the signature.
- Putting an optional parameter before a required one — a compile error with a confusing message.
- Confusing `void` (returns, but nothing) with `never` (doesn't return).

## Menus, not strings

When a value comes from a fixed menu, encode the menu in the type. The modern TypeScript idiom is a **const object + literal union** rather than the `enum` keyword:

```ts
const STATUSES = ['draft', 'review', 'published'] as const;
type Status = 'draft' | 'review' | 'published';

const TRANSITIONS = {
  draft: 'review',
  review: 'published',
  published: 'published',   // terminal state stays put
} as const;

function nextStatus(s: Status): Status {
  return TRANSITIONS[s];
}
nextStatus('draft');     // 'review'
nextStatus('deleted');   // ✗ compile error — not on the menu
```

Line by line: `as const` tells TypeScript to keep the *exact* literals (`'draft'`) instead of widening to `string`. The `TRANSITIONS` object is a **state machine as data**: each status maps to its successor, and the lookup `TRANSITIONS[s]` replaces an if-chain. Because `s` can only be one of the three keys, the lookup never misses — the compiler checks the table covers the union.

The same pattern builds enums of numbers (`const ROLES = { viewer: 1, editor: 2, admin: 3 } as const`), lookup tables of labels, and transition rules. Data-driven beats branch-driven: adding a status means editing a table, not hunting through conditionals.

### Common mistakes

- Typing status fields as `string` "for flexibility" — flexibility here means bugs.
- Scattering transition rules across if-statements in five files instead of one table.
- Forgetting `as const`: without it, `{ draft: 'review' }` has type `{ draft: string }`, losing the literal.

## Two ways to name a type

You've seen `interface` for object shapes. The `type` keyword also names types — including ones interfaces can't express:

```ts
type PostStatus = 'draft' | 'published';          // a union — interface can't do this
type ID = number;                                  // a simple alias

interface User { id: ID; username: string; }       // object shape — either works
type Post = {                                      // same thing with type
  id: ID;
  authorId: ID;
  title: string;
  body: string;
  status: PostStatus;
};

interface Comment extends User { }                 // interfaces extend...
type PostWithAuthor = Post & { author: User };     // ...type uses & (intersection)
```

The practical rules: **unions, tuples, and aliases of primitives need `type`** — `interface` only describes object/function shapes. For plain object shapes, both work and teams split on preference (interfaces give slightly better error messages and can be extended/merged; type aliases are more uniform). Pick one style per codebase and be consistent.

`extends` (interfaces) and `&` intersection (types) both compose shapes: "everything from A plus these fields".

The rest of this lesson models a tiny blog — `User`, `Post`, `Comment` — the way a real CRUD app would, and builds typed functions over it.

### Common mistakes

- Trying `interface Status = 'a' | 'b'` — unions require `type`.
- Redeclaring a shared shape in five files instead of exporting one named type.
- Mixing `extends` and `&` arbitrarily — fine semantically, confusing stylistically.

